   "sha": "09497226dd3f0221a05db2e9eab1d180e7f8b273",
   "node_id": "C_kwDOSVPsWtoAKDA5NDk3MjI2ZGQzZjAyMjFhMDVkYjJlOWVhYjFkMTgwZTdmOGIyNzM",
   "commit": {
    "author": {
     "name": "Contributor",
     "email": "dev@example.com",
     "date": "2026-07-31T02:13:58Z"
    },
    "committer": {
     "name": "Contributor",
     "email": "dev@example.com",
     "date": "2026-07-31T02:13:58Z"
    },
    "message": "fix: remove invented request ceilings; install only the proxy, always latest\n\nRemoves the per-key daily budget added in 4.17.0. A number FCC picks for a\nprovider is a guess, and a wrong guess throttles a key that was fine --\nNVIDIA NIM, for instance, publishes an RPM limit and no daily cap at all.\nKey health should come from what providers actually report, so the tracker,\nits {ENV}_DAILY_LIMIT / {ENV}_QUOTA_RESET_UTC_OFFSET settings, and its docs\nare gone.\n\nWhat stays is the part that reads real signals: a 429's own Retry-After /\nX-RateLimit-Reset window, and skipping a credential whose limiter says it\ncannot serve yet. Both are derived from the upstream response, never invented.\n\nThe installers no longer install Claude Code, Codex, or Pi. They are separate\nthird-party tools that the proxy does not need, and bundling them turned an\nunrelated npm or PATH problem into a failed Free Claude Code install. The\nscript now installs uv and the proxy, and says which launchers exist for\nagents already present.\n\nThey also no longer pin a version or checksum. Both scripts read the latest\nrelease, take the SHA-256 GitHub publishes for that asset, and verify the\ndownload against it -- so the published command always installs the newest\nrelease instead of whatever was current when the docs were written.\n--version / -Version pins an exact release when reproducibility matters.\n\nDocuments Claude Desktop's gateway setup with real screenshots of the\nDeveloper menu and the third-party inference dialog, including the fields the\ndialog actually has today.",
